= Headache
{parent=Neurology}

= Neurological deficit
{parent=Neurology}

= Head injury
{parent=Neurology}

= Traumatic brain injury
{parent=Head injury}

= Concussion
{parent=Traumatic brain injury}

A clinical form of <traumatic brain injury>; confusion and other acute symptoms do not require an observed loss of <consciousness>. <Alcohol intoxication> can coexist with the injury.<Head injury and concussion by the National Health Service>[Symptoms]{ref}<Head injury: assessment and early management by NICE>[§1.4.2]{ref}

= Neurological examination
{parent=Neurology}

= Sensory level
{parent=Neurological examination}

An examination boundary on the body below which a sensory modality changes, useful in localising a <spinal cord> lesion.

= Paralysis
{parent=Neurology}

= Paraplegia
{parent=Paralysis}

= Spinal cord disease
{parent=Neurology}

= Spinal shock
{parent=Spinal cord disease}

An acute phase of depressed reflex activity below severe <spinal cord> injury; it does not establish permanent destruction of all corresponding reflex circuits.

= Haematomyelia
{parent=Spinal cord disease}

= Hematomyelia
{synonym}

Bleeding within the substance of the <spinal cord>, distinguished from bleeding into surrounding spaces.

= Spinal cord infarction
{parent=Spinal cord disease}

= Ataxia
{parent=Neurology}

= Parkinsonism
{parent=Neurology}

= Neuralgia
{parent=Neurology}

Pain attributed to a nerve distribution or nerve disorder.

= Convulsion
{parent=Neurology}

= Cerebral palsy
{parent=Neurology}

A developmental motor disorder associated with disturbances of the developing <brain>. Causes and patterns vary; progressive loss of attained abilities requires reassessment.

= Pseudoparalysis
{parent=Neurology}

Apparent inability to move without necessarily having motor denervation; pain from <osteochondritis> or <periostitis> in <congenital syphilis> is one example.

= Tendon reflex
{parent=Neurology}

= Polyneuropathy
{parent=Neurology}

= Distal symmetric polyneuropathy
{parent=Polyneuropathy}

= Alcohol withdrawal
{parent=Neurology}

= Delirium tremens
{parent=Alcohol withdrawal}

= Delirium
{parent=Neurology}

= Motor impersistence
{parent=Neurology}

Inability to sustain a voluntary posture or action; it can be demonstrated during assessment of <chorea>.

= Chorea
{parent=Neurology}

An involuntary movement pattern; a clinical sign rather than a single cause.

= Sydenham's chorea
{parent=Chorea}
{c}

= Sydenham’s chorea
{c}
{synonym}

An immune-mediated childhood syndrome associated with <acute rheumatic fever>. The name commemorates <Thomas Sydenham>. The neurological syndrome can emerge after the preceding infection is no longer demonstrable.

= Coma
{parent=Neurology}
{disambiguate=medicine}

= Medical coma
{synonym}

A profound sustained impairment of <consciousness> in which a person cannot be awakened. This medical use is distinct from <coma (optics)>.

= Seizure
{parent=Neurology}

= Epilepsy
{parent=Neurology}

= Status epilepticus
{parent=Epilepsy}

= Postictal state
{parent=Epilepsy}

= Epileptic seizure
{parent=Epilepsy}

= Sudden unexpected death in epilepsy
{parent=Epilepsy}

= Hippocampal sclerosis
{parent=Epilepsy}

= Diaschisis
{parent=Neurology}

Dysfunction in a region remote from a lesion because of disrupted interactions with the damaged region. Reduced local activity after <stroke> can therefore reflect more than destruction of the locally measured tissue.

= Dementia
{parent=Neurology}

A syndrome of cognitive decline affecting everyday functioning, arising through different diseases. Age at reported symptom onset, age at diagnosis and the incidence of new cases are distinct outcomes.

= Stroke
{parent=Neurology}

A sudden neurological injury caused by disruption of cerebral blood supply or bleeding. Vascular anatomy can make damage to neighboring tissue and pathways covary, limiting attempts to infer one-to-one mappings from <brain lesions> to computations.

= Cerebral infarction
{parent=Stroke}

= Parkinson's disease
{parent=Neurology}
{c}

A neurodegenerative disorder with motor and nonmotor manifestations, including motivational difficulties in some patients. Comparisons across <dopamine>-related medication states can inform mechanisms but do not selectively manipulate a single cognitive process. The disease name commemorates <James Parkinson>.

= Hemiplegia
{parent=Neurology}

Paralysis affecting one side of the body. Awareness of this impairment and ability to perform movement are distinct clinical questions.

= Neurologist
{parent=Neurology}

= List of neurologists
{parent=Neurologist}

= Lutz Winhuisen
