{c}

Neurologist and researcher on <language> function after <stroke>.

= Work by Lutz Winhuisen
{parent=Lutz Winhuisen}

= Suvarna Alladi
{parent=List of neurologists}
{c}

Neurologist studying cognitive disorders and bilingual experience.

= Work by Suvarna Alladi
{parent=Suvarna Alladi}

= Maria Luisa Gorno-Tempini
{parent=List of neurologists}
{c}
{wiki}

Neurologist studying <language> and <primary progressive aphasia>.

= Work by Maria Luisa Gorno-Tempini
{parent=Maria Luisa Gorno-Tempini}

= Campbell Le Heron
{parent=List of neurologists}
{c}

A <neurologist> whose work investigates <reward>, effort and <apathy>, including experimental choices in <Parkinson's disease>.

= Work by Campbell Le Heron
{parent=Campbell Le Heron}

= Dominique Laplane
{parent=List of neurologists}
{c}

A <neurologist> whose case reports linked severe loss of spontaneous activity with bilateral <basal ganglia> injury and relative preservation under external stimulation.

= Work by Dominique Laplane
{parent=Dominique Laplane}

= John R. Hodges
{parent=List of neurologists}
{c}

A <neurologist> whose studies helped characterize <semantic dementia> as a progressive disorder of <conceptual knowledge>.

= Work by John R. Hodges
{parent=John R. Hodges}

= Alan B. Rubens
{parent=List of neurologists}
{c}

A <neurologist> whose report of <associative visual agnosia> examined visual recognition in relation to copying and other accompanying deficits.

= Work by Alan B. Rubens
{parent=Alan B. Rubens}

= Carl Wernicke
{parent=List of neurologists}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=1848–1905}

A <neurologist> whose work on <aphasia> helped motivate anatomical and functional distinctions within language processing. Clinical syndromes bearing his name must still be explained through specific representations and computations.

\Image[https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/file/Carl_Wernicke.JPG?width=260]
{title=Carl Wernicke, circa 1900}
{width=260}
{source=https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Wernicke.JPG}
{description=Photograph by Max Glauer, credited to Mschr Psychiat Neurol (1906). Wikimedia Commons marks this file public domain.}

= Work by Carl Wernicke
{parent=Carl Wernicke}

= Cognitive neurology
{parent=Neurology}

The study of how neurological disease and injury affect cognition, using clinical patterns and experiments to constrain explanations of normal cognitive function. A <brain lesion> can disrupt connected regions and several computations, so a syndrome is evidence to explain rather than a complete processing model.

= Apathy
{parent=Cognitive neurology}

Reduced goal-directed activity or <motivation> relative to a person’s previous functioning. Initiation, valuation, effort and emotional experience require separate assessment; inactivity is not automatically <anhedonia> or motor incapacity.

= Abulia
{parent=Apathy}

A marked reduction of spontaneous initiative or willful activity. Preserved behavior after prompting can help distinguish impaired self-initiation from inability to execute the same action.

= Auto-activation deficit
{parent=Abulia}

A severe reduction of spontaneous mental and behavioral activity that improves with external stimulation or instruction. Clinical descriptions after bilateral <basal ganglia> injury motivated experiments separating self-activation, incentive translation and motor capacity.<Pure psychic akinesia with bilateral lesions of basal ganglia by Dominique Laplane and others>[1984, externally elicited versus spontaneous behavior]{ref}

= Semantic dementia
{parent=Cognitive neurology}

A progressive syndrome with deterioration of <conceptual knowledge>, often accompanied by fluent <speech>, <anomia> and poor word comprehension. Nonverbal semantic tests are important for distinguishing broad conceptual impairment from an isolated language-output problem.<Non-verbal semantic impairment in semantic dementia by Sasha Bozeat and others>[2000, evidence beyond verbal tests]{ref}

= Visual agnosia
{parent=Cognitive neurology}

Impaired recognition of visually presented objects not adequately explained by elementary visual loss or a general inability to respond. Testing other sensory routes and nonverbal knowledge helps locate the difficulty.

= Associative visual agnosia
{parent=Visual agnosia}

A visual-recognition disorder attributed to difficulty accessing meaning from a relatively adequate visual representation. Copying can provide evidence about <perception>, but line-by-line copying alone does not demonstrate normal integration.<Associative Visual Agnosia by Alan B. Rubens and others>[1971, copying and visual recognition]{ref}

= Apperceptive agnosia
{parent=Visual agnosia}

A visual-recognition disorder in which constructing or integrating an adequate perceptual representation is impaired. The specific perceptual demands must be established rather than inferred from recognition failure alone.

= Anosognosia
{parent=Cognitive neurology}

Impaired awareness of a neurological deficit. Its assessment must distinguish the underlying deficit, a person’s report and evidence that they understand the relevant questions.

= Apraxia
{parent=Cognitive neurology}

Difficulty performing learned actions not adequately explained by elementary weakness, sensory loss or failure to understand the instruction. Different clinically named forms need not share one processing mechanism.

= Dressing apraxia
{parent=Apraxia}

Difficulty organizing dressing actions that can involve relationships between clothing, the body and space. Motor limitations, understanding and neglect require separate assessment.

= Constructional apraxia
{parent=Apraxia}

A traditional clinical term for impaired copying, drawing or assembly of spatially organized material. Failures can involve spatial representations, neglect and planning rather than one general motor-program deficit.

= Optic ataxia
{parent=Cognitive neurology}

Inaccurate visually guided reaching not adequately explained by elementary visual loss or weakness. Comparing gaze, hand and target conditions helps identify the affected visuomotor demands.<Optic ataxia: a specific disruption in visuomotor mechanisms by Marie-Thérèse Perenin and others>[1988, filmed reaching in unilateral-lesion patients]{ref}

= Hemispatial neglect
{parent=Cognitive neurology}

= Unilateral neglect
{synonym}

= Spatial neglect
{synonym}

A disproportionate failure to attend or respond to events on one side of space, often contralesional to <brain> injury. It may affect external space, the body or internally generated scenes and can occur without an equivalent primary visual-field loss.<Unilateral Neglect of Representational Space by Edoardo Bisiach and others>[1978, imagined viewpoints and spatial omissions]{ref}

